00:06:10.266 --> 00:06:25.033
<v Mike Hall>Is there a risk of association for Ruby if thousands of new bootcamp developers come out, and project managers blame Ruby if juniors make mistakes?

00:06:25.033 --> 00:07:03.405
<v Obie Fernandez>Training programs and schools setting expectations correctly helps. Demand for developers keeps growing, with starting salaries of $50k to $70k depending on the market, which is life-changing for many people. On the upper end, salaries between $100k and $200k mean softer factors like culture matter more. That's why I like Hired—they get the salary discussion out of the way first.

00:07:03.405 --> 00:07:18.875
<v Mike Hall>Isn't Hired where they curate candidates to set a minimum bar, so employers put terms upfront and developers don't waste time in long interview processes?

00:07:18.875 --> 00:07:33.340
<v Obie Fernandez>Exactly. You decide who meets your terms upfront so you don't spend weeks interviewing only to find an offer you can't live on in San Francisco.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:48.709
<v Mike Hall>Going back to your early history with Ruby on Rails and the Addison-Wesley Professional Ruby Series—how did you get started with teaching and publishing?

00:07:48.709 --> 00:08:11.713
<v Obie Fernandez>I got pulled into it through blogging. I had a well-read Java blog before leaping to Ruby in 2005 as a loud evangelist. Back then the community was smaller, so it was easier to get heard without relying on Hacker News algorithms.

00:08:11.713 --> 00:08:17.941
<v Mike Hall>I know about the pain of getting people out to read and watch!

00:08:17.941 --> 00:08:35.620
<v Obie Fernandez>Quality content always wins. Over the years, looking at royalty checks, it's amazing how big the Rails community has gotten. It hit the mainstream exceeding our wildest dreams.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:45.063
<v Mike Hall>That's something people in the community might not see—seeing book sales grow year after year.

00:08:45.063 --> 00:08:59.227
<v Obie Fernandez>It grows every year! You come to RailsConf and see companies switching over from .NET and Java, tearing infrastructure out to build on Rails.

00:08:59.227 --> 00:09:06.961
<v Mike Hall>With years of publishing experience, how do you view the shift toward ebooks?

00:09:06.961 --> 00:10:00.000
